{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>It shall be for you a שַׁבָּתוֹן</b> — that it shall be a day of cessation, to rest upon it. And our Rabbis said (Shabbat 24a): \"שַׁבָּתוֹן\" — it is a positive commandment. Now, one who performs labor on a Festival transgresses a negative commandment and a positive commandment, and one who refrains from it fulfills a positive commandment. According to their opinion, all the Festivals are linked to one another, for \"שַׁבָּתוֹן\" is not said regarding the Festival of Matzot nor regarding Shavuot. And in the Mekhilta (Bo 9), I saw in the passage of the New Moon: \"Why is it said, 'And you shall observe this day' (Exodus 12:17)? But was it not already said, 'No labor shall be done in them' (Exodus 12:16)? This I have only for matters that are prohibited on account of labor; for matters that are prohibited on account of shevut, from where? Scripture says, 'And you shall observe this day' — to include matters that are prohibited on account of shevut. Might one say that even chol hamoed should be prohibited on account of shevut? Logic would dictate so! Scripture says, 'On the first day, a שַׁבָּתוֹן' (below 23:39).\" Behold, they derive \"שַׁבָּתוֹן\" to mean ceasing from it entirely, even from things that are not among the avot melakhah (primary categories of labor) and their toladot (subcategories). But this is not clear to me, for if you say it is an asmakhta, what is the reason they would say it in this language? For \"shevut\" in their language is always said regarding their own decrees, and how is it possible to say: things that are prohibited on account of shevut of their decrees — from where in the Torah? And the way of asmakhtot is to state that they are from the Torah, not that they would say: this matter, which is of rabbinic origin, from where in the Torah? Rather, he should have said: things that are not labor — from where? Scripture says \"שַׁבָּתוֹן.\" And it appears that this midrash means to say that we are commanded from the Torah to have rest on a Festival even from things that are not labor — not that one should toil all day measuring grain and weighing fruits and metals and filling wine casks and moving vessels and even stones from house to house and from place to place. And if there were a walled city with gates locked at night, they would load burdens on donkeys, and even wine, grapes, figs, and every kind of cargo they would bring on the Festival, and the market would be full of all buying and selling, and the shop would be open and the grocer weighing out and the money-changers at their tables and the appraisers before them, and the laborers would rise early for their work and hire themselves out like on a weekday for these things and similar to them, and these Festival days would be permitted, and even the Sabbath itself, for none of these involve melakhah. Therefore the Torah said \"שַׁבָּתוֹן\" — that it should be a day of cessation and rest, not a day of toil. And this is a good and fine interpretation. And afterwards I saw in a latter Mekhilta of Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai (12:16) that they taught in different language: \"I have only labor for which one is liable for its sin-offering; labor for which one is not liable for a sin-offering — from where? Such as: one does not climb a tree, nor ride on an animal, nor swim on the surface of the water, nor clap, nor dance. Scripture says: 'all labor.' I have only in a private domain; for a mitzvah — from where? That one does not consecrate, nor evaluate, nor dedicate, nor set aside terumah and tithes — Scripture says: 'שַׁבָּתוֹן' — shevut.\" And of similar import is what is taught in Tanna d'Vei Eliyahu (after chapter 7:9) regarding Yom Kippur. And although these baraitot differ in their language and in their midrashic derivations, perhaps they all intended one thing — to bring an asmakhta for the shevut of rabbinic decree. In any event, whether the interpretation of the first baraita is as we have said, or whether they are all asmakhtot, the meaning of \"שַׁבָּתוֹן\" is this: that we should have rest from toil and exertion, as we have explained. And this is a fitting and very good interpretation. Behold, we were warned about labors on the Sabbath by a negative commandment, with the punishment of karet and death, and about burdensome activities by a positive commandment. And on a Festival, labor is prohibited by a negative commandment, and toil by a positive commandment. And from this the Prophet said (Isaiah 58:13): \"From pursuing your own ways, from finding your own pleasure, and from speaking a word.\" And likewise, \"A Sabbath of solemn rest it shall be for the land\" (Leviticus 25:4) — a Sabbath of rest, that one should neither plow nor work it at all. And this is what they derived — that there is no shevut on chol hamoed, and this is from the Torah. For if it were of rabbinic origin, one would not be permitted to say to a Gentile, \"Do it,\" for they were stringent even regarding shevut of amirah (asking a Gentile to act)." "<b>A remembrance of teruah</b> — verses of Zikhronot and verses of Shofarot, to recall for you the binding of Isaac, in whose place a ram was offered — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 23:24). And the Rav should have also brought the verses of Malkhuyot from the midrash, for it is impossible that Scripture would mention verses of Zikhronot and Shofarot but not mention Malkhuyot. And they have already derived them from the verse \"And they shall be for you for a remembrance before YHWH your Elohim\" (Numbers 10:10), for there is no need for Scripture to say \"I am YHWH your Elohim\" — and what does Scripture come to teach by saying \"I am YHWH your Elohim\"? Rather, this is a prototype: every place where you say Zikhronot, you append to them Malkhuyot, as is found in Tanna d'Vei Eliyahu (parashah 11:2) and in tractate Rosh HaShanah (Rosh HaShanah 32a). But all of this is an asmakhta of rabbinic origin. And it is explicitly stated (Rosh HaShanah 32b): They go to the place where they blow but do not go to the place where they recite blessings. This is obvious — this one is Torah law and this one is rabbinic law; no difficulty arises, for this one is certain and this one is doubtful. But \"a remembrance of teruah\" is like \"a day of teruah it shall be for you\" (Numbers 29:1) — Scripture says that we shall sound the teruah on this day, and it shall be for us a remembrance before HaShem, as is said elsewhere (Numbers 10:10), \"And you shall blow the trumpets, and they shall be for you for a remembrance before your Elohim.\" And because it says there, \"And on the day of your rejoicing and in your appointed seasons, you shall blow the trumpets over your burnt offerings and over your peace offerings,\" whereas here He commanded an undefined teruah for this Festival alone, and afterwards He said (Leviticus 23:11), \"And you shall bring an offering\" — behold, this teruah is not that teruah, for that one is with trumpets over the sacrifice, and this one is not over the sacrifice. Rather, it is an obligation upon all Israel, and it is with the shofar, for He had not yet commanded them to make trumpets, and any undefined teruah is with the shofar, as is said (Leviticus 25:9), \"And you shall sound the shofar of teruah.\" And Scripture did not explain the reason for this commandment — why the teruah, and why a remembrance before HaShem is needed on this day more than on all other days, and why He commands it to be a sacred convocation. But because it falls on the New Moon of Yom Kippur, it appears that on it there is judgment before Him, blessed be He, for \"He judges the peoples\" on it; on Rosh HaShanah \"He sits on the throne of justice,\" and afterwards, during the Ten Days, \"He bears the sin of His servants\" — Scripture hinted at this matter as it became known in Israel from the mouth of the Prophets and holy fathers. And by way of truth, the teruah is that which stood for our forefathers and for us, as is said, \"Happy is the people that knows the teruah\" (Psalms 89:15), in the sense of what is written, \"the teruah of battle\" (Jeremiah 4:19), for HaShem is a man of war. If so, \"a day of teruah it shall be for you\" — that the day shall be for us as a teruah. And likewise, \"a remembrance of teruah, a sacred convocation\" — that the remembrance shall be by means of teruah, and therefore it is a sacred convocation. And there was no need to mention the shofar, for the shofar is hinted at in \"day\" and the \"teruah\" within it, and behold, it is a day of judgment in mercy, not a teruah of battle. And for this reason Scripture mentioned the teruah, for it is already received in the hands of our Rabbis and all Israel, seeing back to Moses our teacher, that every teruah has a plain blast before it and a plain blast after it. And why would Scripture mention teruah and not mention the tekiah blasts at all — neither on Rosh HaShanah nor on Yom Kippur? But the tekiah is the remembrance, and it is the shofar, and the teruah is as its name. And because it is included within mercy — a tekiah before it and after it. Therefore it says regarding \"those who know the teruah\" that \"in Your name they rejoice all the day,\" \"for the glory of their strength are You.\" And behold, this makes it clear that everything depends on repentance — only on Rosh HaShanah He unites with the attribute of judgment and conducts His world, and on Yom Kippur with the attribute of mercy. And this is their statement (Rosh HaShanah 32a): \"The King sits on the throne of judgment,\" etc. Rosh HaShanah is a day of judgment with mercy, and Yom Kippur is a day of mercy within judgment. And from the matter that we have explained, you will understand the Scripture's reason in the passages of the journeys (Numbers 10:6–7): \"A teruah you shall blow for their journeys,\" and \"when assembling the congregation, you shall blow but not sound a teruah\" — for in their journeys it is written, \"And the angel of Elohim journeyed\" (Exodus 14:19), and it is written, \"And Your enemies shall scatter, and those who hate You shall flee from before You\" (Numbers 10:35), in the sense of \"the face of YHWH is against evildoers\" (Psalms 34:16); and when assembling the congregation it is said (Numbers 10:36), \"Return, YHWH, to the myriads,\" in the sense of \"And there was a king in Jeshurun, when the heads of the people were gathered together\" (Deuteronomy 33:5). And the Ten Days between Rosh HaShanah and Yom Kippur allude to the ten Sefirot, for on Yom Kippur He is elevated in them and exalted, YHWH of Hosts, in judgment, as is known in Kabbalah. And there is also a sign for this in the heavens, for this month's zodiac sign is Libra, for in it HaShem sets the scales and balances of justice."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "The abbreviation ת\"כ (Tanna Kamma or Tanna d'Vei Eliyahu) is resolved here as Tanna d'Vei Eliyahu based on Ramban's typical citation practice and the cross-reference given.", 1 "The verse cited as Psalms 89:15 appears with a paraphrased continuation blending nearby verses (vv. 16–17); rendered literally as found in the Hebrew text of the comment."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:24:45.730247266Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}